  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Unrestricted Cash Assistance for Flood-Affected Populations in Bayelsa State, Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria has been in the grip of the worst flood crisis in the last decade. The destructive flooding has affected more than 3.2 million people and caused massive damage to people&#x2019;s homes and community infrastructure. The floods destroyed farmlands, shelters, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) infrastructures, sources of livelihood, and caused further displacement of populations. According to the International Organization for Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) and National Emergency Management Agency data from December 2022, a total of 368,187 people were affected and over 400 houses damaged in Bayelsa. This project targets flood-affected populations, including displaced populations, returnees and host communities in Bayelsa State, Nigeria. Direct assistance is provided in the form of cash and voucher assistance, promoting immediate choice prioritization, dignity, and flexibility. Providing the flood-affected population with cash helps them determine and settle underlining unexpressed needs. Moreover, cash distribution is faster, proven to be an adaptable means of saving lives, more efficient and a reliable modality to provide immediate lifesaving needs. This project complements IOM&#x2019;s other ongoing flood response interventions in Bayelsa which includes damage assessment, shelter and non-food item interventions, psychosocial support, protection, and provision of farming kits.</narrative>
  </description>
